{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of silence that only exists at 6 a.m. It isn't empty. It's full of potential, like a blank page or a kitchen before anyone has made coffee. Most of us sleep right through it, trading it for an alarm that drags us straight into noise.\n\nI've been trying to reclaim that silence. Not in a disciplined, five-a.m.-club, cold-plunge way. Just by sitting with it. No phone, no journal, no agenda to optimize my morning. I sit on the floor by the window and watch how the light decides to enter the room. It never crashes in. It seeps, slowly, touching the edge of the bookshelf first, then the chipped mug on the table, then finally my socks.\n\nWe're taught to look for the big moments \u2014 graduations, promotions, heartbreaks \u2014 as if life only happens in headlines. But a life is really made of these small migrations of light. The way a barista remembers you like oat milk. The way your dog sighs before settling. The way an old song on the grocery store radio can carry you back ten years in four notes.\n\nIf you pay attention, the world is constantly whispering. The question is whether we've left any quiet inside ourselves to hear it.\n\nMaybe happiness isn't about adding more, but about noticing more of what's already there. And perhaps that quiet noticing is the most honest form of gratitude we can practice each day without saying a single word aloud to anyone else.",
